<h2>Abstract</h2>

<p>In May 2026, Google published a blog post stating that users &quot;love&quot; AI Mode in Google Search. In the week that followed, DuckDuckGo — a privacy-focused search engine that does not serve AI-generated answers by default — saw its desktop traffic surge by nearly 28%. This paper examines the event through a Human-AI Fit lens, analyzing three signals: user demand for control over AI outputs, the value of search transparency, and the contemporary validation of Locus of Control theory in HCI design.</p>

<h2>The Event: A Contradiction in Data</h2>

<p>In mid-May 2026, Google posted on its Search Central blog that user engagement with AI Mode was &quot;significantly elevated.&quot; One week later, multiple independent trackers reported that DuckDuckGo's desktop traffic had surged by nearly 28% (PC Gamer, May 2026).</p>

<p>Two data points published within the same week, pointing in opposite directions: Google says users love AI search, and users are migrating to a search engine that deliberately avoids AI-generated answers.</p>

<p>This is not an isolated critique of Google. It reveals something deeper — while AI product designers rush to &quot;do everything for the user,&quot; a measurable segment of users are making the opposite choice with their behavior.</p>

<h2>Why This Matters: The Return of Search Transparency</h2>

<p>DuckDuckGo founder Gabriel Weinberg has long maintained a principle: search is about <strong>information retrieval</strong>, not <strong>information generation</strong>. When a user types a query, DuckDuckGo returns links — not an AI-synthesized block of text that &quot;looks like&quot; an answer.</p>

<p>Data supports his stance. A 2026 survey by the Annenberg Public Policy Center found that over 60% of respondents could not determine how reliable AI-generated search results were. 40% said they trusted raw source links more than AI summaries.</p>

<p>DuckDuckGo's follow-up move after the traffic surge is telling: it announced it would frame AI-generated result summaries with a <strong>red border</strong> and a disclaimer — &quot;This summary was generated by AI and may contain errors.&quot; This is not anti-AI. It is AI <strong>marked, bounded, and made optional</strong>.</p>

<h2>Theoretical Lens: Locus of Control and Human-AI Fit</h2>

<p>This phenomenon maps closely onto the classic HCI concept of <strong>Locus of Control</strong> (Lefcourt, 1982): when users perceive a loss of control over a tool, satisfaction and trust decline — even if the tool performs well objectively.</p>

<p>Google AI Mode's fundamental change is that it <strong>chooses for the user</strong> — it reads multiple sources, generates a summary, and draws conclusions. For a meaningful subset of users, this is not efficiency — it is a loss of agency.</p>

<p>The post &quot;I'm Tired of Talking to AI&quot; — which received 1,869 points on Hacker News in May 2026 — captures the extreme version of this experience. The author described asking AI about a GitHub security issue, getting a wrong answer, then posting on GitHub and receiving a human reply that was <em>verbatim identical</em> to the AI's wrong answer. He had been answered by another human acting as an AI relay.</p>

<p>This is the terminal stage of control loss — you can no longer tell whether you're talking to a person or an intermediary for a machine.</p>

<h2>Looking Ahead: An Alternative Model for Human-AI Fit</h2>

<p>DuckDuckGo's 28% surge is not an outlier. It reveals a long-overlooked dimension of AI product design: <strong>users want to retain choice and control</strong>.</p>

<p>The prevailing Silicon Valley narrative holds that AI should be as human-like as possible — more proactive, more autonomous, more capable of doing everything for the user. But DuckDuckGo's data suggests that a nontrivial segment of users want a <strong>tool</strong>, not an <strong>agent</strong>.</p>

<p>For Human-AI Fit researchers, this may be the most instructive &quot;counter-case&quot; of 2026: a search engine with no AI credentials grew against the AI tide. It tells us that Human-AI Fit is not just about making AI more capable — it is also about making AI <strong>visible, bounded, and optional</strong>.</p>

